1 LINK
    The LINK command links object modules (.OBJ files, such as MACRO
    writes) into a VAX/VMS executable image (.EXE) that RUN can run, or
    into a shareable image (/SHAREABLE) whose routines and data other
    images use.  Its images and maps are laid out as VMS's LINK lays them
    out, so an image made here runs on VMS, and VMS's images link here.

    Syntax:

        LINK <file>[,<file>...][/HOST] [/EXECUTABLE[=<image>] |
             /SHAREABLE[=<image>] | /NOEXECUTABLE] [/SYMBOL_TABLE[=<file>]]
             [/[NO]TRACEBACK] [/[NO]DEBUG] [/[NO]SYSLIB]
             [/[NO]USERLIBRARY[=(<table>[,...])]]
             [/MAP[=<map>] [/BRIEF | /FULL] | /NOMAP]

    Parameter:

        <file>          An object file (.OBJ), or a file with its own
                        qualifier, which follows it:

            <library>/LIBRARY
                        An object library (.OLB) searched for symbols the
                        objects don't define, before the system libraries,
                        or a shareable image library (LIBRARY/SHARE, or
                        IMAGELIB).  A shareable image library's images are
                        looked for beside it, then in SYS$LIBRARY, then as
                        RUN finds them.
            <library>/INCLUDE=(<module>[,...])
                        Adds those modules of the library to the image:
                        of a shareable image library, those images, as an
                        options file's /SHAREABLE does.  With /LIBRARY
                        too, the library is also searched.
            <object>/SELECTIVE_SEARCH
                        Takes only the object's definitions of symbols
                        something else refers to.
            <options>/OPTIONS
                        An options file (.OPT); see below.

                        Files follow MACRO's rules for host files and
                        files on mounted volumes (see HELP MACRO), and a
                        later file's bare name is found beside the one
                        before it.  /HOST applies to every file.

    Qualifiers:

        /EXECUTABLE[=<image>]
                        Writes the image.  This is the default.  Without
                        a file name, the image has the first object's
                        name and location, with the type EXE in the same
                        case as the object's type.  A name that is only a
                        directory gets that name in it.

        /SHAREABLE[=<image>]
                        Makes a shareable image: one with no transfer
                        address, linked at address 0 so that RUN can put
                        it anywhere, whose universal symbols (the options
                        UNIVERSAL= and the objects' .TRANSFER) are in its
                        global symbol table for other links to use.  The
                        image is named as /EXECUTABLE names it (one or the
                        other may name it, not both).  Its options file
                        sets its ident and match control (GSMATCH=); see
                        "Shareable images" below.

        /NOEXECUTABLE   Links and reports any errors, but writes no image.

        /SYMBOL_TABLE[=<file>]
                        Also writes the image's global symbol table as an
                        object module file (default: the first object's
                        name with the type STB), which another link can
                        take as an object.

        /TRACEBACK      Starts the image through SYS$IMGSTA, as VMS LINK
                        does by default, and gives it a debug symbol
                        table built from the objects' traceback records.
                        /NOTRACEBACK starts it at its own transfer
                        address, with no debug symbol table.

        /DEBUG          Puts the objects' debugger records (from
                        MACRO/DEBUG) in the debug symbol table too, and
                        marks the image for the debugger, as VMS LINK
                        does.  /DEBUG includes /TRACEBACK, even if
                        /NOTRACEBACK is given.  RUN starts such an
                        image under the debugger (see HELP RUN).  A
                        user-written debugger module (/DEBUG=<file>)
                        isn't supported.  /NODEBUG is the default.

        /SYSLIB         Searches IMAGELIB.OLB and STARLET.OLB for symbols
                        the objects don't define, as VMS LINK does by
                        default.  /NOSYSLIB uses only govax's own tables.

        /USERLIBRARY[=(<table>[,...])]
                        Searches the default user libraries, after the
                        command's own libraries and before the system's:
                        the object or shareable image libraries the
                        logical names LNK$LIBRARY, LNK$LIBRARY_1, ...
                        LNK$LIBRARY_999 name, taken in turn until one
                        isn't defined.  The tables are PROCESS, GROUP, and
                        SYSTEM, searched in that order; ALL, the default,
                        is all three.  /NOUSERLIBRARY (or NONE) searches
                        none.

        /MAP[=<map>]    Writes a link map, laid out as VMS LINK lays out
                        its default map: the object modules, the program
                        sections and each module's part of them, the
                        global symbols the objects define, and the image
                        synopsis.  Without a file name, the map has the
                        first object's name and location, with the type
                        MAP.  /NOMAP, the default, writes none.

        /BRIEF          Makes the map brief: only the object modules and
                        the image synopsis.

        /FULL           Makes the map full: it also lists the image
                        sections (the Image Section Synopsis: each
                        cluster's sections, their types, and a shareable
                        image's global section names, matches, and idents),
                        and the program sections and symbols the libraries
                        and shareable images gave.

    An options file has a line for each option or list of input files. An
    exclamation point starts a comment, and a hyphen at a line's end
    continues it.  Its files may have /LIBRARY, /INCLUDE, and
    /SELECTIVE_SEARCH, and also /SHAREABLE, a shareable image (.EXE) whose
    symbols the image uses, found as RUN would find it (see HELP RUN), so
    a logical name of its name or SYS$SHARE leads to it.  A host path is
    written in quotes, for its slashes.  SYS$INPUT/OPTIONS reads the
    options from SYS$INPUT: in a command procedure, the data lines after
    the LINK command.  Options and keywords may be abbreviated.  The
    options are VAX LINK's:

        STACK=<pages>           The user stack's first size (default
                                20).  With paging on it grows past
                                that by itself, to about 10 MB with
                                the defaults; with paging off the
                                stack is a fixed 2 MB of P1, and
                                STACK= is ignored.
        IDENTIFICATION=<id>     The image's identification.
        NAME=<name>             The image's name in its header.
        SYMBOL=<name>,<value>   Defines a global symbol, whatever the
                                objects say.
        UNIVERSAL=<name>[,...]  Makes symbols universal: a shareable
                                image's symbols other images may use.
        GSMATCH=<match>,<major>,<minor>
                                A shareable image's match control (ALWAYS,
                                EQUAL, or LEQUAL) and ident; RUN checks it
                                (see HELP RUN).  Without it the ident comes
                                from the link time, and the match is
                                EQUAL, so a program must be relinked when
                                the image is.
        CLUSTER=<name>[,<base>][,<pfc>][,<file>...]
                                A cluster of image sections: the files
                                named, placed together and in this order,
                                at an address if <base> is given.
        COLLECT=<cluster>,<psect>[,...]
                                Moves program sections into a cluster.
        PSECT_ATTR=<psect>,<attribute>[,...]
                                Changes a program section's attributes
                                (PIC, SHR, WRT, EXE, RD, GBL, ... and their
                                NO forms, or an alignment).
        BASE=<address>          The image's base address.  An executable
                                linked at a base places its shareable
                                images at link time too.
        DZRO_MIN=<pages>        The fewest pages of a writable section's
                                uninitialized run that become a demand-zero
                                section of their own.
        ISD_MAX=<count>         The most image sections an executable may
                                have before demand-zero runs stay with
                                their sections (ignored, with OPTIGNSHR, for
                                a shareable image).
        PROTECT=YES|NO          Marks the image sections of the clusters
                                that follow as protected, as VMS needs for
                                a shareable image installed /PROTECT
                                (govax has no INSTALL; RUN ignores it).
        IOSEGMENT=<pages>[,[NO]P0BUFS]
                                The image I/O segment's size.

    UNSUPPORTED= is refused.  Numbers are decimal, or hexadecimal or octal
    after %X or %O.  A bad option or value is %LINK-F-OPTSYNERR or
    OPTVALERR, quoting it.

    Program sections are laid out as VMS LINK lays them out: grouped into
    image sections by their attributes, from address 200 (hexadecimal) in
    an executable and 0 in a shareable image, cluster by cluster: the
    options' clusters, then the default cluster, then a cluster for each
    shareable image.  Writable pages nothing is stored in become
    demand-zero.

    Shareable images:

    A library of routines linked /SHAREABLE has, in the usual pattern, a
    transfer vector first, so that its routines keep their addresses from
    one version of the image to the next:

        .TRANSFER   MY_ROUTINE          ; MY_ROUTINE is universal
        .MASK       MY_ROUTINE          ; the routine's entry mask
        JMP         L^MY_ROUTINE+2      ; and a jump past it

    with an options file such as

        CLUSTER=TRANSFER_VECTOR,,,VECTOR
        GSMATCH=LEQUAL,1,1000
        UNIVERSAL=MY_DATA

    A program uses it by naming it in an options file (MYSHR/SHAREABLE),
    and reaches its routines and data with general mode operands
    (CALLS #0,G^MY_ROUTINE) or a .ADDRESS of them.  The image's GSMATCH=
    decides which later versions the program accepts: with LEQUAL, any
    with the same major id and a higher or equal minor id.  A shareable
    image may itself use others.  LIBRARY/CREATE/SHARE makes a library of
    shareable images, which LINK searches for the images that define
    symbols (see HELP LIBRARY).

    A global symbol the objects don't define is looked up, in order, in:

        IMAGELIB.OLB    Which shareable image defines the routine.  Its
                        offset there comes from the image itself
                        (SYS$SHARE:LIBRTL.EXE, say) or, with no such
                        file, from govax's own tables.
        STARLET.OLB     The module that defines the symbol is added to
                        the image, as VMS LINK does.
        govax's tables  Every LIBRTL routine, and the other run-time
                        library routines govax provides (DECC$..., and
                        others), in their shareable images; STARLET's
                        status codes and other definitions (SS$_...,
                        RMS$_..., IO$_..., and more); and the system
                        services (SYS$...), at their addresses in the P1
                        vector.

    The two libraries and the shareable images are VMS files, which govax
    doesn't include.  LINK looks for them as SYS$LIBRARY:IMAGELIB.OLB,
    SYS$LIBRARY:STARLET.OLB, and SYS$SHARE:<image>.EXE on a mounted
    volume, then in the host directory the vax.library setting names
    (or, if that isn't set, the older vax.link.library).  Without them,
    govax's own tables, which were captured from those files, give the
    same image for everything they define.  Running a routine still
    needs its image, or govax's own routine for it.

    A routine in a shareable image is reached with a general mode operand
    (CALLS #1,G^LIB$PUT_OUTPUT) or a .ADDRESS of it; the image reaches it
    through its fixup section, and RUN connects it to the image, or for
    LIBRTL and the system's other images, to govax's own routines (see
    HELP RUN).

    A symbol nothing defines is a warning, as in VMS LINK: it is 0, each
    reference to it is reported, and the image is still written.  A
    symbol referred to only weakly (.WEAK) is 0 without a warning.  An
    image with no transfer address is written with a warning too.

    With /TRACEBACK, the objects' traceback records become the image's
    debug symbol table, after the image's other blocks, as VMS LINK
    writes it.  The debugger reads it (SHOW CALLS names each routine and
    line), and the image runs on VMS with VMS's symbolic traceback; govax
    itself doesn't print a traceback yet when a program fails, only the
    condition's message.  Debugger records (from MACRO/DEBUG) are skipped
    unless the link is /DEBUG.

    From the host shell, "govax link <object>..." runs this command, with
    --executable <file>, --shareable, --no-executable, --symbol-table
    <file>, --no-traceback, --debug, --no-syslib, --no-userlibrary, --map,
    --map-file <file>, --brief, --full, --library <file>, or --options
    <file>.

    Examples:

        MACRO "hello.mar"
        LINK "hello"
        RUN "hello.exe"

        LINK DUA0:[OBJ]MAIN,SUB /EXECUTABLE=DUA0:[EXE]PROG.EXE
        LINK "hello"/MAP
        LINK MAIN,MYLIB/LIBRARY,PROG/OPTIONS

        LINK/SHAREABLE=MYSHR/MAP/FULL VECTOR,ROUTINES,MYSHR/OPTIONS
        DEFINE MYSHR DUA0:[WORK]MYSHR.EXE
        LINK MAIN,SYS$INPUT/OPTIONS     ! in a procedure, then the line:
        MYSHR/SHAREABLE
        RUN MAIN

    See also the help for MACRO and RUN.
